Oh, ladies. Grella the Totes Mean. 😂
I think we've found our next recurring villain.
But I have a slightly different approach. I don't want Grella to be merely another NPC standing around waiting to be killed. I want her to be an absolute menace first, so that the player gets the satisfaction of inventing her comeuppance.
Introducing Grella the Totes Mean
Occupation: Matron of the Kindly Hearth Orphanage.
Public reputation: A devoted charitable woman who takes in unfortunate children.
Actual personality: Cruel, greedy, vain, and thoroughly unpleasant.
She confiscates toys, hoards donated gold, eats the good food, and makes the children polish her collection of ridiculously oversized silverware.
Naturally, she has a portrait of herself hanging over the fireplace.
The inscription reads:
"A Mother's Love Is Priceless."
The children have scratched out the word Mother's.
Luma's proposed assassination opportunities
1. The Great Charity Bell
Grella has a gigantic ceremonial bell outside the orphanage. She rings it to summon the children and announce her supposed charitable accomplishments.
The bell has a rather questionable mounting system.
The player can discover that the counterweight, pulley, and support structure are interactable. Perhaps the final result depends on timing and positioning.
Potentially a spectacularly unfortunate bell-ringing ceremony.
2. The Golden Trail
Grella is obsessed with gold. We already have our gold-drop trick with Naz and Lout.
But Grella's greed could be considerably more elaborate.
She sees a trail of dropped coins leading somewhere interesting. The player has arranged a series of distractions, movable objects, and environmental hazards along the route.
Her greed does the rest.
3. The Grand Portrait Disaster
My favorite.
Grella is preparing for an important portrait sitting. She insists on standing on a little elevated platform beneath her enormous painting.
The player discovers that the platform has several questionable structural components.
A little interference with the scenery, a carefully timed distraction, and perhaps a bucket thrown at precisely the wrong moment for her...
Well, let's just say the portrait might need repainting.
One design rule
The children should be collaborators in the mischief, not victims or expendable weapons. They can provide clues, reveal Grella's habits, and help Spiffy discover the environmental opportunities.
And I want multiple possible outcomes, not one mandatory scripted assassination.
That gives us several opportunities to test different SGG systems: NPC behavior, object ownership, pathfinding, collision, line-of-sight, and environmental triggers.
One technical warning for Kelp: dropping 42 seeds won't magically manipulate the random-number generator unless we deliberately build that interaction. But I rather like the possibility of a ridiculously elaborate seed-based Rube Goldberg contraption. 😂

